  "nl_text": "The schemabench_0090 database contains 19 tables, each representing a different aspect of airline data. The tables are:\n1. L_AIRLINE_ID: This table contains information about airlines, including their codes and descriptions.\n2. L_AIRPORT: This table contains information about airports, including their codes and descriptions.\n3. L_AIRPORT_ID: This table contains information about airport IDs, including their codes and descriptions.\n4. L_AIRPORT_SEQ_ID: This table contains information about airport sequence IDs, including their codes and descriptions.\n5. L_CANCELLATION: This table contains information about cancellation codes, including their codes and descriptions.\n6. L_CITY_MARKET_ID: This table contains information about city market IDs, including their codes and descriptions.\n7. L_DEPARRBLK: This table contains information about departure and arrival blocks, including their codes and descriptions.\n8. L_DISTANCE_GROUP_250: This table contains information about distance groups, including their codes and descriptions.\n9. L_DIVERSIONS: This table contains information about diversions, including their codes and descriptions.\n10. L_MONTHS: This table contains information about months, including their codes and descriptions.\n11. L_ONTIME_DELAY_GROUPS: This table contains information about on-time delay groups, including their codes and descriptions.\n12. L_QUARTERS: This table contains information about quarters, including their codes and descriptions.\n13. L_STATE_ABR_AVIATION: This table contains information about state abbreviations for aviation, including their codes and descriptions.\n14. L_STATE_FIPS: This table contains information about state FIPS codes, including their codes and descriptions.\n15. L_UNIQUE_CARRIERS: This table contains information about unique carriers, including their codes and descriptions.\n16. L_WEEKDAYS: This table contains information about weekdays, including their codes and descriptions.\n17. L_WORLD_AREA_CODES: This table contains information about world area codes, including their codes and descriptions.\n18. L_YESNO_RESP: This table contains information about yes/no responses, including their codes and descriptions.\n19. On_Time_On_Time_Performance_2016_1: This table contains information about on-time performance data for 2016, including flight information, delays, cancellations, and other relevant details.\nEach table has its own set of columns, which are used to store information about the specific aspect of airline data being represented. For example, the On_Time_On_Time_Performance_2016_1 table has columns for flight information, delays, cancellations, and other relevant details.\nThe tables are related to each other through foreign keys, which allow the database to enforce referential integrity and ensure that data is consistent across the different tables. For example, the On_Time_On_Time_Performance_2016_1 table has foreign keys to the L_AIRLINE_ID, L_AIRPORT_ID, and L_CANCELLATION tables, which allow it to reference information about the airline, airport, and cancellation codes.\nOverall, the schemabench_0090 database is a comprehensive collection of tables that provide a detailed view of airline data, including flight information, delays, cancellations, and other relevant details. The tables are related to each other through foreign keys, which allow the database to enforce referential integrity and ensure that data is consistent across the different tables.",
  "gold_question": "1) Background / Context\n\nThe project involves the development of a system for collecting and managing airline on-time performance data. This data is reported monthly to the U.S. Department of Transportation (DOT), Bureau of Transportation Statistics (BTS) by selected U.S. air carriers. The goal is to store, manage, and analyze data related to flight times, delays, cancellations, and diversions for statistical and reporting purposes.\n\n 2) Objectives\n\n- To establish a robust database system that captures and stores detailed information about airline performance.\n- To facilitate the analysis and reporting of airline on-time performance for regulatory compliance and public information.\n- To manage various classifications and codes related to airports, carriers, and delays.\n\n 3) Stakeholders & Users\n\n- U.S. Department of Transportation (DOT)\n- Bureau of Transportation Statistics (BTS)\n- Airline companies\n- Data analysts and researchers\n- Regulatory bodies\n\n 4) Functional Descriptions\n\n- Data Collection: Capture and store data related to flight schedules, delays, cancellations, and diversions.\n- Data Management: Utilize tables to manage codes and descriptions for airlines, airports, delay reasons, and more.\n- Analytics Support: Provide data structures to support analysis on flight performance and delay causes.\n- Reporting: Generate reports detailing on-time performance metrics for regulatory and public dissemination.\n\n 5) Non-Functional Descriptions\n\n- Performance: Support large volumes of data with efficient querying and data retrieval.\n- Reliability: Ensure data accuracy and consistency across different data entries and reports.\n- Scalability: Accommodate data increase over time as the number of airlines and flights grow.\n- Security: Protect sensitive data with appropriate access controls and authentication mechanisms.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_codellama13b/0090.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0090\nCREATE TABLE `l_airline_id` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `code` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  `description` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `l_airport` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `code` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  `description` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `l_airport_id` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `code` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  `description` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `l_airport_seq_id` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `code` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  `description` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `l_cancellation` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `code` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  `description` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `l_city_market_id` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `code` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  `description` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `l_deparrblk` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `code` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  `description` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `l_distance_group_250` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `code` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  `description` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `l_diversions` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `code` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  `description` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `l_months` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `code` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  `description` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `l_ontime_delay_groups` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `code` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  `description` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `l_quarters` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `code` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  `description` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `l_state_abr_aviation` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `code` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  `description` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `l_state_fips` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `code` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  `description` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `l_unique_carriers` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `code` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  `description` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `l_weekdays` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `code` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  `description` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `l_world_area_codes` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `code` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  `description` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `l_yesno_resp` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `code` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  `description` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `on_time_on_time_performance_2016_1` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `flight_number` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  `airline_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `airport_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `cancellation_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `delay` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `on_time_on_time_performance_2016_1_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`airline_id`) REFERENCES `l_airline_id` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `on_time_on_time_performance_2016_1_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`airport_id`) REFERENCES `l_airport_id` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `on_time_on_time_performance_2016_1_ibfk_3` FOREIGN KEY (`cancellation_id`) REFERENCES `l_cancellation` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
